1
00:00:01,180 --> 00:00:08,030
On a quiet stretch of the Rhine, a friend kept seeing them: small dimples on the water,

2
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:10,520
drifting beside the boat.

3
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:13,910
Some lasted a surprisingly long time.

4
00:00:14,460 --> 00:00:17,720
Each one is the top of a whirlpool.

5
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:26,040
Water spinning in a circle needs something pushing it inward, and in water, that push is pressure,

6
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:28,430
lower at the centre.

7
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:31,560
So the surface sags into a shallow funnel.

8
00:00:32,110 --> 00:00:35,040
But a river is chaos.

9
00:00:34,980 --> 00:00:38,790
Everything in it gets sheared and torn.

10
00:00:38,730 --> 00:00:42,890
So how does one tiny whirlpool hold itself together?

11
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:48,270
Dive under one, and it's not a point at all.

12
00:00:48,210 --> 00:00:53,140
It's a line: a thread of spinning water running down into the river.

13
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:59,500
A line meets the surface in a single point.

14
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,640
That point is the dimple.

15
00:01:03,690 --> 00:01:07,500
And these lines obey a strange rule.

16
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:15,430
In 1858, Hermann von Helmholtz proved that a vortex line can't just stop inside the water.

17
00:01:15,980 --> 00:01:17,620
Try it.

18
00:01:17,560 --> 00:01:21,860
Put a loop around the line and slide it off the end.

19
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:28,140
The loop never crosses any spin, so its total swirl can't change.

20
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:34,280
But past the end, the loop can shrink to nothing, and nothing has no swirl.

21
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:42,720
So a vortex line has to close into a loop, or end on something: the riverbed, or the surface.

22
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:50,770
Which is exactly what a smoke ring is: a vortex line, closed into a loop.

23
00:01:53,520 --> 00:02:01,210
In 1867, in Edinburgh, Peter Guthrie Tait built a box that fired smoke rings,

24
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:05,990
and showed it to his friend William Thomson, later Lord Kelvin.

25
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:14,570
The rings bounced off each other, wobbled, and survived their collisions.

26
00:02:14,510 --> 00:02:21,890
Helmholtz had even predicted that two rings travelling together would take turns passing through each other.

27
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:28,010
Kelvin was captivated.

28
00:02:27,950 --> 00:02:36,180
By Helmholtz's rules, a vortex in a perfect fluid could never be created or destroyed.

29
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:41,790
So maybe atoms were smoke rings in the ether, and each element a different knot.

30
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:47,240
He was wrong about atoms.

31
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:54,850
But Tait spent years cataloguing knots to test the idea, and helped start the mathematics of knot theory.

32
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:01,040
And a smoke ring explains the Rhine.

33
00:03:00,980 --> 00:03:06,150
Push a ring up into the surface, and since it can't end in the water,

34
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:09,470
it joins onto the surface instead.

35
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:17,100
The ring opens into an arch with two ends: two dimples, spinning opposite ways.

36
00:03:20,150 --> 00:03:24,191
That's why dimples so often come in pairs.

37
00:03:24,131 --> 00:03:29,241
Each rides the other's swirl, and the pair sails off together.

38
00:03:31,591 --> 00:03:37,021
But there are no smoke boxes on the Rhine, and hardly any paddles.

39
00:03:36,961 --> 00:03:39,051
So what's making them?

40
00:03:39,601 --> 00:03:41,571
The riverbed.

41
00:03:41,511 --> 00:03:48,691
Water touching the bottom is held still, while the current races past just above.

42
00:03:48,631 --> 00:03:54,801
That shear rolls up into arches, like half smoke rings, lifting off the bed.

43
00:03:55,351 --> 00:04:02,191
When one reaches the surface, it joins on, and its two legs become a pair of dimples.

44
00:04:04,741 --> 00:04:08,151
From then on, it's a tug of war.

45
00:04:08,091 --> 00:04:12,241
Friction slowly spreads a whirlpool out.

46
00:04:12,181 --> 00:04:19,201
But where surface water converges and sinks, it stretches the whirlpool and spins it back up.

47
00:04:19,751 --> 00:04:24,101
And the dimples you notice are only the lucky ones.

48
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:31,281
A dimple is barely steeper than a ripple, so on a breezy day they're still there, just hidden.

49
00:04:32,431 --> 00:04:37,371
There's one more reason they can last, and it's about dimensions.

50
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:42,531
In three dimensions, turbulence works by stretching.

51
00:04:42,471 --> 00:04:49,421
Pull a spinning tube longer, and it spins faster and thinner, until it frays apart.

52
00:04:49,971 --> 00:04:57,571
Right at the surface, water can hardly move up or down, so the flow there is nearly flat.

53
00:04:57,511 --> 00:05:01,381
And a flat flow has nothing to stretch.

54
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:06,731
Instead of tearing apart, whirlpools tend to merge into bigger ones.

55
00:05:09,281 --> 00:05:17,821
It's part of why storms in Jupiter's thin, flattened atmosphere can merge into giants like the Great Red Spot.

56
00:05:18,371 --> 00:05:25,241
Take that flatness all the way, as a model, and the whirlpools obey Hamilton's rule,

57
00:05:25,181 --> 00:05:29,121
the same mathematics as a swinging pendulum.

58
00:05:29,061 --> 00:05:33,011
The river's surface becomes its own phase space.

59
00:05:34,161 --> 00:05:38,661
But real rivers aren't perfectly flat underneath.

60
00:05:38,601 --> 00:05:43,411
Water wells up and sinks, and that's where it gets interesting.

61
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:50,821
A team in Trondheim, Norway, led by Omer Babiker and Simen Ellingsen,

62
00:05:50,761 --> 00:05:54,671
found that the number of dimples on a patch of water

63
00:05:54,611 --> 00:05:58,751
tracks how much its surface is spreading and converging:

64
00:05:58,691 --> 00:06:03,501
first in simulations, and this year in a laboratory tank.

65
00:06:04,051 --> 00:06:12,521
And that churning helps set how fast a river breathes, trading oxygen and carbon dioxide with the air.

66
00:06:13,071 --> 00:06:21,421
So the dimples beside the boat were the ends of vortex lines, half smoke rings risen from the riverbed.

67
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:28,101
And a friend who noticed how long they lasted was already, in a way, reading the river.
